    "text": "The dispatch office hummed with the low, constant breath of the building\u2019s heating system. It was a sound so pervasive it had become a kind of silence, a blanket over the deeper quiet of the 2:17 AM lull. Outside the grimy window, the rail yard was a study in black and amber, lines of freight cars sleeping under the sodium lamps.\n\nArthur had been a dispatcher for thirty-one years. The screens in front of him were his world, a constellation of green dots and numbered blocks that represented the lifeblood of the region. He knew the rhythm of the rails better than his own heartbeat. The 2:45 to Fulton was running seven minutes late, a minor arrhythmia in the night\u2019s steady pulse.\n\nTo pass the time, he pulled a thick, dog-eared book from his desk drawer. It was a timetable from 1988, a relic from when he\u2019d first started. The paper was soft as cloth, the ink faded. He didn\u2019t need it, not really. The old schedules were ghosts, their routes long since redrawn or abandoned. But he liked the feel of it, the tangible history.\n\nHe was flipping through the pages, past the long-defunct \u201cPinecrest Local\u201d and the \u201cNorthern Flyer,\u201d when a small, folded piece of paper slipped from between the pages for the \u201cKestrel Valley Line.\u201d It wasn't the brittle, yellowed paper of the timetable. This was a scrap of lined notebook paper, the kind a schoolchild would use.\n\nHe unfolded it carefully",
